Operationalizing the Translation Gap

A Design‑Science Framework with the AIPS Specification Language and Adversarial Auditing

Author: Ouadi Maakoul


Abstract

Enterprise artificial intelligence suffers from a persistent structural failure: the Translation Gap—a misalignment between a business stakeholder’s intent and the machine‑learning problem that data‑science teams actually solve. Neither industrial practice nor academic research has yet produced a formal, computable mechanism for detecting, measuring, and reducing this gap before models are built. This design‑science PhD proposal presents a complete theoretical, linguistic, and architectural solution. The contributions are: (1) an operational distance estimator \hat{d} that avoids circularity by combining preference constraint violations under a surrogate business model with round‑trip semantic fidelity and causal structural checks; (2) the AI Problem Specification Language (AIPS)—formally defined through a BNF grammar, denotational semantics mapping business constructs to Markov Decision Process components, and decidable invariant checking—deliberately distinguished from ISO 18629 (Process Specification Language); (3) a four‑agent cooperative‑adversarial architecture (APFE) that includes semantic grounding, template‑based problem generation, and an argumentation‑mining critic trained on a purpose‑built synthetic dataset of specification flaws; (4) a protocol for adapting existing business simulation environments as lightweight surrogates for pre‑deployment policy testing; (5) a three‑arm randomised controlled trial design with gold‑standard specifications and automated metrics to test both causal efficacy and human‑AI trust calibration; and (6) a concrete open‑standard adoption strategy centred on an MLflow plugin. All elements are presented at the level of mathematics, architecture, and technical specification; no implementation or empirical data collection has yet been performed.

---

1. Introduction

The paradox of modern enterprise AI is that the exponential growth in model capability has been accompanied by an accelerating rate of project failure. In 2025, 42 % of enterprises abandoned the majority of their AI initiatives, up from 17 % in 2024 (S&P Global). Root‑cause analyses conducted by the RAND Corporation and by Trabelsi et al. (2026) point unequivocally to problem formulation as the most frequent failure antecedent: organisations routinely solve a well‑defined problem that does not correspond to the business need they intended to address.

We call this mismatch the Translation Gap. It is a structural gap between the true business problem P and the problem \hat{P} that a data‑science team formulates and solves. Because the team then optimises a solution S(\hat{P}) against \hat{P} rather than P, the realised business value can approach zero regardless of the technical quality of the model.

Existing approaches to AI project initiation—stakeholder brainstorming, exploratory dashboards, canvas methods—are human‑intensive, non‑formal, and unverifiable. No current system can automatically validate whether a problem statement is well‑defined, align business objectives with machine‑learning objectives, or systematically generate and critically evaluate alternative formulations before modelling begins.

This PhD proposes a design‑science remedy: a complete framework for operationalising the Translation Gap, comprising a formal specification language, an operational gap metric, a multi‑agent system for problem framing, and rigorously specified evaluation protocols. The work is entirely theoretical and architectural; it delivers mathematics, language design, system blueprints, and experimental designs that are ready for implementation and empirical testing.

Version 7.0 incorporates critical advisory feedback. The specification language has been renamed AIPS to avoid conflict with the established ISO 18629 Process Specification Language, and its novelty relative to that standard is explicitly drawn. The surrogate business model methodology is repositioned as an adaptation of existing simulation techniques. The randomised trial is instrumented with gold‑standard references and automated metrics. The synthetic training dataset generation is detailed with a validation plan. The standards adoption strategy is made concrete through a named integration target.

