2. The Translation Gap: Formalisation and Operational Estimator

2.1 Theoretical Gap

Definition (True Business Problem).
P = (U, \Theta, C, \mathcal{A}), where:

· U is the latent utility function of the business over outcomes,
· \Theta represents external states (market conditions, competitor actions),
· C is a set of real‑world constraints (regulatory, budgetary, operational),
· \mathcal{A} is the space of possible interventions or decisions.

Definition (Formulated Problem).
\hat{P} = (\hat{U}, \hat{\Theta}, \hat{C}, \hat{\mathcal{A}}), the data‑science team’s surrogate, typically a simplified model with proxy metrics, idealised constraints, and a restricted action space.

Let S(\hat{P}) be the solution produced by optimising or learning from \hat{P} (e.g., a trained model plus decision rule). The realised utility is V(S(\hat{P}), P), i.e. the performance of the deployed solution in the true environment. The Translation Gap is

\Delta(P, \hat{P}) = V(S(P), P) - V(S(\hat{P}), P).

Because the true P is unknown, \Delta cannot be computed directly. The challenge is to build an operational estimator that is computable from observable artefacts and whose behaviour correlates strongly with the unobservable gap.

2.2 Operational Estimator \hat{d}

We define \hat{d}(P, \hat{P}) using three observable quantities, each with explicitly bounded validity.

Component 1 – Preference Constraint Violations (PrefViol).
The stakeholder provides a set \mathcal{R} of hard and soft outcome preferences (e.g., “profit must not fall by more than 5 %”, “customer churn rate ≤ 3 %”). Given a formulated problem \hat{P}, we extract its implied optimal policy \pi_{\hat{P}} (via an MDP solver or a lightweight optimisation routine). We then evaluate this policy on a surrogate business model—a lightweight simulation environment constructed by adapting existing simulation techniques (see §5). For a sample of N trajectories, PrefViol is the fraction in which \pi_{\hat{P}} violates any constraint in \mathcal{R}.

Assumption: The surrogate is accurate enough to expose gross misalignments. False positives are possible if the surrogate is poor, but the risk is mitigated by sensitivity analysis over surrogate parameters.

Component 2 – Round‑Trip Semantic Fidelity (RTSF).
Rather than measuring paraphrase stability (which rewards consistent but potentially wrong translations), we check whether the formal objective can be translated back into natural language and judged equivalent to the original business statement. Let \llbracket \cdot \rrbracket be the denotation of a specification in AIPS (see §3), and let g_{\text{NLG}} be a template‑driven natural‑language generation function that produces a business‑facing description of the specification. A panel of domain experts (or, for Phase 1 calibration, a validated BERT‑based similarity metric) rates the semantic similarity between the original statement and the back‑translation on a normalised scale. RTSF is the average similarity over k back‑translation variants.

Assumption: The back‑translation and similarity evaluation are reasonably faithful; we validate the metric on a small labelled set in Phase 1.

Component 3 – Causal Structural Soundness (CausalCoh).
For any AIPS specification that includes a causal DAG, we automatically check acyclicity and the validity of back‑door adjustment sets using the dagitty graph library. LLM‑based domain plausibility ratings are collected but not weighted in the primary metric, preserving objectivity.

Combined Estimator:

\hat{d}(P, \hat{P}) = \alpha \cdot \text{PrefViol}(\hat{P}, \mathcal{R}) \;+\; \beta \cdot (1 - \text{RTSF}(\hat{P})) \;+\; \gamma \cdot (1 - \text{CausalCoh}(\hat{P}))

Weights \alpha, \beta, \gamma will be calibrated in Phase 1 by regressing the components against sponsor‑rated outcomes (1–5 scale) for 50–80 historical AI projects. As a robustness check, results will also be reported with uniform weights and with expert‑elicited weights.

