3. AI Problem Specification Language (AIPS)

3.1 Rationale and Differentiation from ISO 18629

The proposed language is deliberately named AIPS (AI Problem Specification Language) to avoid confusion with the existing ISO‑standardised Process Specification Language (PSL, ISO 18629), which was developed by NIST to model manufacturing and business processes. Whereas ISO 18629 describes sequences of activities, their preconditions, and their effects, AIPS is a declarative, optimisation‑oriented language. It describes what an AI system should maximise, minimise, maintain, or detect, under explicit constraints, within a defined time horizon—and then maps these declarations directly onto the components of a Markov Decision Process. AIPS is thus complementary to process languages; it governs the objective function of an AI model, not the workflow around it.

AIPS also differs from earlier AI‑elicitation languages and from ad‑hoc YAML‑based ML specification formats by providing formal denotational semantics and automated invariant verification.

3.2 Concrete Syntax (Excerpt)

```bnf
<spec>           ::= <header> <body>
<header>         ::= "spec" <id> "version" <semver>
<body>           ::= <objective> <domain> <actions> <constraints> <data_req>
<objective>      ::= "objective" ":" <verb> <metric> <entity> <polarity> <horizon>
<verb>           ::= "maximize" | "minimize" | "maintain" | "detect" | "explain"
<metric>         ::= <identifier> "." <property> | "custom(" <expr> ")"
<entity>         ::= "over" <identifier> "in" <cohort>
<polarity>       ::= "positive" | "negative"
<horizon>        ::= <number> <time_unit>
<domain>         ::= "domain" ":" <identifier> "ontology" <uri>
<actions>        ::= "actions" ":" <action_list>
<action_list>    ::= <action> ("," <action>)*
<action>         ::= <identifier> "type" ("binary" | "continuous" | "discrete")
<constraints>    ::= "constraints" ":" <constraint_list>
<constraint_list>::= <constraint> ("," <constraint>)*
<constraint>     ::= <expr> [ "severity" ("hard"|"soft" <weight>)]
<data_req>       ::= "data" ":" <data_block>
<data_block>     ::= "tables" ":" <table_ref>+ "freshness" <duration>
```

3.3 Formal Semantics

The semantics of an AIPS specification are given by a denotation function \llbracket\cdot\rrbracket that maps it to a Markov Decision Process \mathcal{M} = (\mathcal{S}, \mathcal{A}, \mathcal{T}, \mathcal{R}_{\hat{U}}, \mathcal{C}):

· \mathcal{S}: state space, derived from the data schema and domain ontology.
· \mathcal{A}: action space, taken directly from the <actions> block.
· \mathcal{T}: transition function, left abstract; it is assumed that the environment dynamics will be estimated from data or provided by the surrogate.
· \mathcal{R}_{\hat{U}}: reward function, constructed compositionally from the <objective> block. For example, the phrase minimize churn.probability over customers in high_risk_segment negative horizon 90 days translates to \mathcal{R}(s,a) = -(\text{churn\_probability}(s') + \lambda \cdot \text{cost}(a)), with the horizon encoded via a discount factor \gamma = e^{-1/90}.
· \mathcal{C}: constraints, encoded as forbidden action sequences or state‑action penalty terms according to severity.

Invariant Verification. An AIPS specification is well‑formed if its denotation produces an MDP with a non‑empty constrained action space for all states reachable under the declared constraints. We use an SMT solver (Z3) over a finite abstraction to check:

· No two hard constraints are mutually unsatisfiable.
· All soft constraints have weight sums consistent with the objective.
· All referenced metrics are defined in the domain ontology or data schema.
  The existence of the underlying data tables is an operational concern, not a semantic property; the verifier emits a warning but does not reject the specification.

3.4 Worked Examples

Example 1 – Well‑formed AIPS.

```
spec churn-prediction version 1.0.0
objective: minimize churn.probability over customers in high_risk_segment negative horizon 90 days
domain: telecom ontology https://schema.org/Business
actions: send_retention_offer type binary
constraints: cost_per_offer <= 5.0 severity hard,
              churn_rate_change >= -0.02 severity soft 0.8
data: tables: customer_activity, historic_churn freshness 7 days
```

Denotation yields a valid MDP; invariant checker passes.

Example 2 – Failing type check.

```
spec faulty version 1.0.0
objective: maximize revenue.custom("f(x)") over unknown_segment positive horizon 60 days
domain: finance ontology https://spec.openapis.org/finance
actions: trade_volume type string
constraints: trade_volume > "high" severity hard
data: tables: market_data freshness 1 day
```

Error output:

```
TypeError: 'trade_volume' declared as string, but constraint uses numeric comparison.
Warning: unknown_segment not found in data tables.
```

These examples demonstrate the immediate practical value of the formal syntax and type system.

