6. Adversarial Critic and Training Data Strategy

The critic’s performance hinges on the quality of its training data. We will construct two datasets:

6.1 SynFail‑2K (Synthetic Failure Corpus)

Generation mechanism:

1. Start from a set of 50 ground‑truth correct AIPS specifications paired with their business GoalSpecs (created by the research team and validated by two domain experts).
2. For each correct specification, apply a set of mutation operators designed to introduce realistic Translation‑Gap errors:
   · Proxy substitution: replace the primary metric with a correlated but non‑identical proxy (e.g., replace customer_lifetime_value with quarterly_revenue).
   · Horizon mismatch: shift the optimisation horizon to a shorter period than the business need.
   · Constraint weakening: remove a soft constraint or reduce its weight.
   · Entity scope error: change the over cohort to a subset or superset.
3. Use a large language model (GPT‑4) to rewrite the mutated specification into superficially coherent AIPS text, preserving the introduced flaw.
4. For each flawed specification, automatically generate an argument graph using a template‑based parser, then manually correct it to ensure that the flaw is reflected as a missing or rebutted premise.

Validation: A random sample of 200 SynFail‑2K entries will be reviewed by domain experts to confirm that the injected flaws are realistic and non‑trivial. Inter‑annotator agreement will be reported.

6.2 RealFail‑50 (Real‑World Mis‑specification Set)

Fifty real project post‑mortems that contain documented formulation errors will be manually annotated with AIPS specifications and argument graphs by the researcher in consultation with industry partners. This dataset serves as a held‑out test set to evaluate transfer from synthetic to real cases.

6.3 Critic Training and Benchmarking

The critic (DeBERTa‑v3‑base) will be fine‑tuned on SynFail‑2K with a multi‑task objective: (Task A) edge classification in argument graphs; (Task B) regression to predict an expert‑rated alignment score. After training, we will benchmark its misalignment detection accuracy against a prompted large general‑purpose model (GPT‑4) on the RealFail‑50 set. Our hypothesis is that the specialised model outperforms the generalist on this narrow, high‑stakes task.

