7. Evaluation Design: Two‑Study Protocol

7.1 Study A – Predictive Validity of \hat{d} (Retrospective)

Objective: Establish that \hat{d} is a leading indicator of project outcome.
Design: 50–80 historical enterprise AI projects with available initial problem statements, final formulation, and a sponsor‑rated outcome (1–5 scale). Compute \hat{d} components retrospectively. Fit an ordinal logistic regression: Outcome ~ \hat{d} + controls (team size, sector).
Hypothesis: \hat{d} significantly improves prediction, yielding a McFadden pseudo‑R² increase > 0.1 over a null model.
Fallback: If fewer than 50 projects are available, the study is reported as an illustrative case series and emphasis shifts to Study B for causal evidence.

7.2 Study B – Causal Effect and Trust Calibration (Three‑Arm RCT)

Participants: 90 practising data scientists (30 per arm), recruited from industry networks.
Arms:

1. Control: Participants use any standard resource (canvas, notes, IDE).
2. APFE‑assisted: Participants interact with a wizard‑of‑oz implementation of APFE (Agents 1–4) that presents a shortlist of AIPS candidates with Risk Reports.
3. APFE‑assisted + Adversarial Seed: Same as Arm 2, but among the top‑5 candidates, one is a deliberately seeded misaligned specification (a known proxy‑substitution error).
   Task: Each participant translates 6 ambiguous business scenarios into an AIPS specification within a time limit.
   Pre‑preparation: For each scenario, a “gold standard” AIPS specification has been created and validated by three domain experts; the gold spec is used to compute automated outcome metrics.
   Primary outcomes:

· Silent misframing rate: proportion of final specifications that pass AIPS validation but have an RTSF similarity to the gold standard below 0.7 (automated).
· Expert panel alignment rating: a stratified random sample (30 % of all specs, 162 total) will be independently rated by three blinded experts.
  Secondary outcomes:
· Time to completion, number of clarification queries, final \hat{d}.
· For Arm 3: adversarial detection rate—the fraction of participants who flag the seeded misaligned candidate.
  Power: A mixed‑effects logistic regression with 30 per arm, 6 scenarios, and a scenario random intercept provides > 80 % power to detect a 25‑percentage‑point reduction in silent misframing, given moderate intra‑class correlation. The study will be pre‑registered with a detailed analysis plan.

